OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026", "title": "The irrationality exponent of pi is 2", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "017", "family_title": "The irrationality exponent of <i>π</i> is 2", "abstract_html": "We prove the conjecture that the irrationality exponent of <i>π</i> is 2. As a consequence, the classical Flint–Hills series \\(\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)\\) converges, with angles in radians.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The irrationality exponent of \\(\\pi\\) is \\(2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi\\) denote the usual positive circle constant.</p><p class=\"li\">For an irrational real number \\(x\\), its irrationality exponent is\n  \\[\n  \\mu(x)=\\sup\\left\\{\\nu&gt;0:\n  0&lt;\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;q^{-\\nu}\n  \\text{ for infinitely many coprime }p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ q\\ge2\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The irrationality exponent of \\(\\pi\\) is \\(2\\). More precisely, for every real \\(\\nu&gt;2\\), there is an integer \\(Q(\\nu)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|\\pi-\\frac pq\\right|\\ge q^{-\\nu}\n\\qquad\n(p\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ q\\ge Q(\\nu)).\n\\]\nThe inequality applies whether or not \\(p/q\\) is reduced. The threshold may depend on \\(\\nu\\), and the argument does not give it effectively.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\nu&gt;2\\). Suppose for a contradiction that there are arbitrarily large positive integers \\(q\\) for which some \\(p\\in\\mathbb Z\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\left|\\pi-\\frac pq\\right|\\le q^{-\\nu}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(\\theta,A,B,C,\\eta,g\\) from Proposition 3, and put\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_0=\\min(g/2,1/2).\n\\]\nFirst choose \\(F_0&gt;2/\\theta\\) so large that\n\\[\n\\nu/F_0&lt;\\varepsilon_0/3.\n\\]</p><p>For an integer \\(m\\), set\n\\[\nK=\\lfloor C^m\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\nw_0=B^{-m},\n\\qquad\nv_0=2K\\theta^m w_0.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThese are admissible rational interpolation data:\n\\[\nK\\theta^m\\le(C\\theta)^m&lt;1,\n\\qquad\nK\\frac{w_0}{v_0}\\theta^m=\\frac12.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nAs \\(m\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\frac K{w_0}\\le(CB)^m\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\nv_0\\sim2\\left(\\frac{C\\theta}{B}\\right)^m\\longrightarrow\\infty,\n\\qquad\n\\frac m{v_0}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nWith the notation of Proposition 2,\n\\[\nL\n=\n\\frac{\\eta^2K\\theta^m}{(m+1)v_0A^m}\n=\n\\frac{\\eta^2(B/A)^m}{2(m+1)}\n\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p><p>Thus, with \\(\\Lambda=4\\log2\\) and \\(c=(\\log2)/4\\), choose \\(m\\) large enough that\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Lambda F_0m+2\\log2}{v_0}\n+\\frac{100K}{w_0}\n&lt;\n\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{3},\n\\qquad\ncL&gt;2.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nFix this \\(m\\), and hence \\(K,w_0,v_0\\).</p><p>The remaining constants are now fixed. Choose approximations \\(p_i/q_i\\) satisfying (4.3), with\n\\[\nw_i=\\lceil\\log q_i\\rceil,\n\\]\nsuccessively sufficiently large for Proposition 1 and also so that\n\\[\n\\Lambda\\sum_{i=1}^m\\frac1{w_i}\n+\n\\frac{\n\\theta+\\log4+\\log(2K)+\\nu+\\log(200K)\n}{w_*}\n&lt;\n\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{3},\n\\qquad\nw_*=\\min_{i&gt;0}w_i.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nThis is possible: first impose a sufficiently large lower bound on every \\(w_i\\), using\n\\[\n\\sum_i1/w_i\\le m/w_*,\n\\]\nand then impose the successive geometric thresholds. The assumed denominators are unbounded, so every finite sequence of such requirements can be met.</p><p>The thresholds for weight separation are independent of the centers. For sufficiently large \\(q_i\\), one has \\(p_i\\ne0\\), and therefore\n\\[\nc_{ji}=jr_i,\\qquad r_i=2\\mathrm i p_i/q_i\n\\]\nare distinct across \\(j\\) in each coordinate. No independence between different coordinates is required.</p><p>The error sum in Proposition 2 is exactly\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}\n={}&amp;\n\\frac{\\nu}{F_0}\n+\\frac{\\Lambda F_0m+2\\log2}{v_0}\n+\\frac{100K}{w_0}\\\\\n&amp;+\n\\Lambda\\sum_i\\frac1{w_i}\n+\n\\frac{\n\\theta+\\log4+\\log(2K)+\\nu+\\log(200K)\n}{w_*}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe three choices make this less than \\(\\varepsilon_0\\). In particular,\n\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}&lt;g,\n\\qquad\ncL&gt;2&gt;1+E_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}.\n\\]\nAll hypotheses of Proposition 2 now hold, a contradiction. Its degree \\(H\\) tends to infinity only after the dimension, weights, and centers have been fixed.</p><p>Thus (4.3) is excluded for arbitrarily large \\(q\\), for each fixed \\(\\nu&gt;2\\). This gives the required eventual lower bound, in fact with a strict inequality.</p><p>The contradiction allowed unreduced fractions and zero error. If \\(\\pi\\) were rational, exact representations with arbitrarily large denominators would contradict it. Hence \\(\\pi\\) is irrational.</p><p>Finally, divide the unit interval into \\(N\\) equal parts and apply pigeonhole to\n\\[\n0,\\pi,\\ldots,N\\pi\n\\]\nmodulo one. There exist \\(p\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and \\(1\\le q\\le N\\) such that\n\\[\n0&lt;\\left|\\pi-\\frac pq\\right|\n\\le\\frac1{qN}\\le\\frac1{q^2}.\n\\]\nAfter reduction, the same error is at most the inverse square of the reduced denominator. These errors tend to zero as \\(N\\to\\infty\\); irrationality forces the reduced denominators to be unbounded. Every exponent less than two therefore occurs infinitely often in the defining inequality. Hence \\(\\mu(\\pi)\\ge2\\), and the upper bound already proved gives\n\\[\n\\mu(\\pi)=2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The classical Flint–Hills series \\( \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\frac{1}{n^3\\sin^2 n} \\) converges.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Angles are measured in radians.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The classical Flint–Hills series\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\frac{1}{n^3\\sin^2 n}\n\\]\nconverges.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a real number \\(\\nu\\) with \\(2&lt;\\nu&lt;5/2\\), and choose a positive integer \\(Q\\) as in Theorem 1. Taking a nearest integer numerator gives\n\\[\n\\|q\\pi\\|\\ge q^{1-\\nu}\\qquad(q\\ge Q).\n\\]\nTheorem 1 has also established irrationality of \\(\\pi\\). Hence\n\\[\nc=\n\\min\\left(\n\\{1\\}\\cup\n\\{q^{\\nu-1}\\|q\\pi\\|:q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ 1\\le q&lt;Q\\}\n\\right)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\|q\\pi\\|\\ge cq^{1-\\nu}\n\\]\nfor every positive integer \\(q\\), including the finitely many exceptions below \\(Q\\). Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{q=1}^{\\infty}\\frac1{q^3\\|q\\pi\\|^2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p><p>For each positive integer \\(n\\), choose a nearest nonnegative integer \\(q\\) to \\(n/\\pi\\), and group the integers \\(n\\) by \\(q\\). Then\n\\[\n|n-q\\pi|\\le\\pi/2.\n\\]\nThe group with \\(q=0\\) is finite, and its terms are finite because irrationality of \\(\\pi\\) excludes \\(\\sin n=0\\) for a positive integer \\(n\\).</p><p>For each \\(q\\ge1\\), its group lies in an interval of length \\(\\pi&lt;4\\), so contains at most four integers. Every integer in that group satisfies\n\\[\nn\\ge\\pi(q-1/2)\\ge\\frac{\\pi q}{2}.\n\\]\nConcavity of sine on \\([0,\\pi/2]\\) gives\n\\[\n|\\sin n|\n=\n|\\sin(n-q\\pi)|\n\\ge\n\\frac2\\pi|n-q\\pi|\n\\ge\n\\frac2\\pi\\|q\\pi\\|.\n\\]\nThe contribution of the group indexed by \\(q\\ge1\\) is therefore at most\n\\[\n\\frac8\\pi\\,\\frac1{q^3\\|q\\pi\\|^2}.\n\\]\nSumming this bound and using (5.1) proves convergence.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^a|\\sin n|^b} \\) converges if and only if \\( a>\\max\\{1,b\\}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a,b&gt;0\\) be fixed real numbers.</p><p class=\"li\">Angles are measured in radians.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The series\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^a|\\sin n|^b}\n\\]\nconverges if and only if\n\\[\na&gt;\\max\\{1,b\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose first that\n\\[\na&gt;\\max\\{1,b\\}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) so that\n\\[\na&gt;\\max\\{1,b\\}+b\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nTheorem 1, with its finitely many exceptional denominators absorbed into a positive constant as in the proof of Theorem 2, gives\n\\[\n\\|q\\pi\\|\\ge c_\\varepsilon q^{-1-\\varepsilon}\n\\qquad(q\\ge1).\n\\]</p><p>For an integer \\(K\\ge1\\), put\n\\[\nd=c_\\varepsilon(2K)^{-1-\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nAs in the proof of Proposition 4, the points \\(q\\pi\\) on \\(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) with \\(K\\le q&lt;2K\\) are mutually \\(d\\)-separated and at least \\(d\\) from zero. There are at most \\(K\\) points, so the half-circle ordering gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{K\\le q&lt;2K}\\frac1{q^a\\|q\\pi\\|^b}\n&amp;\\le\n2K^{-a}d^{-b}\\sum_{j=1}^K j^{-b}\\\\\n&amp;\\ll_{a,b,\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{cases}\nK^{1-a+b\\varepsilon},&amp;0&lt;b&lt;1,\\\\\nK^{1-a+\\varepsilon}\\log(2K),&amp;b=1,\\\\\nK^{b-a+b\\varepsilon},&amp;b&gt;1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach power of \\(K\\) is negative by the choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\). Summing over \\(K=1,2,4,\\ldots\\), including the logarithmic factor when \\(b=1\\), proves\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\ge1}q^{-a}\\|q\\pi\\|^{-b}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Use the same nearest-multiple groups as in the proof of Theorem 2. The \\(q=0\\) group is finite, while the group indexed by \\(q\\ge1\\) contributes at most\n\\[\n4(2/\\pi)^a(\\pi/2)^b q^{-a}\\|q\\pi\\|^{-b}.\n\\]\nThis proves convergence when \\(a&gt;\\max\\{1,b\\}\\).</p><p>If \\(a\\le1\\), including \\(a=1\\), then \\(|\\sin n|\\le1\\) compares the series from below with the divergent series \\(\\sum n^{-a}\\).</p><p>It remains to consider \\(1&lt;a\\le b\\). Let \\(p_j/q_j\\) be the continued-fraction convergents to \\(\\pi\\), whose positive integer numerators tend to infinity. They satisfy\n\\[\n|\\sin p_j|\n=\n|\\sin(p_j-\\pi q_j)|\n\\le\n|p_j-\\pi q_j|\n&lt;\nq_j^{-1}.\n\\]\nSince \\(p_j/q_j\\to\\pi\\), eventually\n\\[\np_j\\le(\\pi+1)q_j.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac1{p_j^a|\\sin p_j|^b}\n&gt;\np_j^{-a}q_j^b\n\\ge\n(\\pi+1)^{-b}p_j^{b-a}.\n\\]\nThese summands are bounded away from zero when \\(a=b\\), and tend to infinity when \\(a&lt;b\\). Thus the series diverges throughout \\(1&lt;a\\le b\\), including the boundary \\(a=b&gt;1\\).</p><p>Therefore convergence holds exactly when \\(a&gt;\\max\\{1,b\\}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, “Separated-weight interpolation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are successive lower thresholds for positive rational numbers \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\), depending only on the fixed data and previously chosen weights, with the following property.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix integers \\(m,K\\ge1\\) and positive rational numbers \\(w_0,v_0,\\theta\\) such that\n  \\[\n  0&lt;\\theta&lt;1,\\qquad\n  K\\theta^m&lt;1,\\qquad\n  K\\frac{w_0}{v_0}\\theta^m&lt;1.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All multi-indices have nonnegative integer entries.</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive rational vector \\(W=(w_0,\\ldots,w_m)\\), the \\(W\\)-degree of\n  \\(Y^hX_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots X_m^{\\alpha_m}\\) is\n  \\[\n  w_0h+\\sum_{i=1}^m w_i\\alpha_i.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\mathcal P_W(H)\\) be the complex vector space of polynomials of \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive rational vector \\(V=(v_0,\\ldots,v_m)\\), let \\(\\mathcal J_V(H)\\) be the quotient of\n  \\(\\mathbb C[[t,u_1,\\ldots,u_m]]\\) by the ideal of series whose monomials all have \\(V\\)-weight at least \\(H\\). Its retained coefficients are indexed by\n  \\[\n  v_0s+\\sum_{i=1}^m v_i\\beta_i&lt;H.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The expression \\(\\log(1+t)\\) denotes its power series at zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are successive lower thresholds for positive rational numbers \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\), depending only on the fixed data and previously chosen weights, with the following property.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nW=(w_0,w_1,\\ldots,w_m),\\qquad\nV=(v_0,w_1/\\theta,\\ldots,w_m/\\theta).\n\\]\nFor any points\n\\[\na_j=(1,c_{j1},\\ldots,c_{jm})\\in\\mathbb C^{m+1},\n\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;K,\n\\]\nsuch that \\(c_{0i},\\ldots,c_{K-1,i}\\) are pairwise distinct for each \\(i\\), the map\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_W(H)\\longrightarrow\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{K-1}\\mathcal J_V(H),\n\\qquad\nP\\longmapsto\n\\left(\nP\\left(1+t,(c_{ji}+u_i+\\log(1+t))_{i=1}^m\\right)\n\\right)_{j=0}^{K-1}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nis surjective for all sufficiently large, sufficiently divisible integers \\(H\\).</p><p>The divisibility requirement can be chosen using only the weights. The remaining lower threshold for \\(H\\) may depend on the points.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\sigma\\) and the successive weights as in Lemma 1.2. We pass from its curve inequality to the coefficient packets in (2.2), using ordinary projective schemes and the ordinary blow-up. Smoothness or normality of the compactification is not needed.</p><p>Choose an integer \\(R\\) sufficiently large that \\(R/w_a\\) and \\(R/v_a\\) are positive integers for every index \\(a\\). Embed affine space by all monomials of \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(R\\), including the constant monomial, and let \\(X\\) be the projective closure of the image. Since these monomials include each coordinate, the chart of the constant monomial is precisely the original affine space. Let \\(L=\\mathcal O_X(1)\\), a very ample line bundle.</p><p>For any curve meeting this chart,\n\\[\n\\deg(L|_C)=R\\deg_W C.\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nOn its smooth complete model, at each point clear the largest pole of the homogeneous monomial coordinates. Every monomial has pole order at most \\(R\\) times the maximum in (2.10). A pure power of one coordinate attains that bound whenever the maximum is positive, and the constant monomial attains it when the maximum is zero. The resulting sections have no common zero, proving (2.17).</p><p>To make the local data algebraic, replace \\(\\log(1+t)\\) in each coordinate by a fixed Taylor polynomial \\(G_i(t)\\) whose omitted terms have \\(t\\)-weight strictly greater than \\(v_i\\). Then\n\\[\nt=Y-1,\\qquad\nu_i'=X_i-c_{ji}-G_i(t)\n\\]\nare regular local coordinates at \\(a_j\\).</p><p>This replacement preserves \\(h_P\\). If \\(t\\) is not identically zero on a branch, each omitted term has order divided by \\(v_i\\) strictly larger than \\(\\operatorname{ord}_P(t)/v_0\\); if \\(t\\) is identically zero, its tail vanishes. It also preserves interpolation surjectivity for every threshold: the formal coordinate change fixes \\(t\\) and changes \\(u_i\\) only by terms of weight greater than \\(v_i\\). It and its inverse preserve the weighted filtration and induce inverse triangular maps on each finite coefficient packet.</p><p>At \\(a_j\\), form the finite-colength ideal\n\\[\nI_j=\\left(t^{R/v_0},(u_1')^{R/v_1},\\ldots,(u_m')^{R/v_m}\\right).\n\\]\nThe points are distinct. These local ideals, together with the unit ideal away from the points, define a coherent ideal sheaf \\(I\\) on \\(X\\). For a branch through a center,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_P I\n=\n\\min_a\\left\\{(R/v_a)\\operatorname{ord}_P z_a\\right\\}\n=\nRh_P,\n\\qquad z_0=t,\\quad z_i=u_i'.\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(p:X'\\to X\\) be the ordinary blow-up of \\(I\\), the relative Proj of its Rees algebra, and write\n\\[\nI\\mathcal O_{X'}=\\mathcal O_{X'}(-E).\n\\]\nThe scheme \\(X\\) is integral and Noetherian, \\(I\\) is coherent and nonzero, and \\(E\\) is an effective Cartier divisor. The tautological bundle \\(\\mathcal O_{X'}(-E)\\) is relatively ample; these are the blow-up properties cited in [22, Tags 02OS, 02ND, and 02NS].</p><p>Write \\(A=p^*L\\) in additive divisor notation. We claim\n\\[\nA-(1+\\sigma)E\\quad\\text{is nef}.\n\\tag{2.19}\n\\]\nFor a noncontracted integral curve \\(\\Gamma\\) upstairs whose image meets the affine chart, the map to its image is birational, since \\(p\\) is an isomorphism away from the finite center. On the complete normalizations, (2.17) and (2.18) give\n\\[\nA\\cdot\\Gamma=R\\deg_W p(\\Gamma),\n\\qquad\nE\\cdot\\Gamma=R\\sum_{P\\mapsto a_j}h_P.\n\\]\nLemma 1.2 gives the required nonnegative degree. A curve whose image misses the affine chart avoids the exceptional divisor. A contracted curve has \\(A\\)-degree zero and positive degree for \\(-E\\) by relative ampleness. These cases account for every integral curve, proving (2.19).</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(a&gt;1\\), the divisor \\(aA-E\\) is ample. Choose \\(b\\) so that \\(I\\otimes L^b\\) is globally generated. The Rees construction embeds \\(X'\\) in a product \\(X\\times\\mathbb P^r\\), with the second hyperplane bundle restricting to \\(bA-E\\). Tensoring with the first very ample bundle makes \\((b+1)A-E\\) ample.</p><p>Now\n\\[\nA-E\n=\n\\frac{a-1}{a(1+\\sigma)-1}\\bigl(A-(1+\\sigma)E\\bigr)\n+\n\\frac{\\sigma}{a(1+\\sigma)-1}(aA-E).\n\\tag{2.20}\n\\]\nThis expresses \\(A-E\\) as a positive multiple of a nef class plus a positive multiple of an ample class. It is therefore ample, by [11, Corollary 1.4.10], which applies to projective schemes and does not require a smooth blow-up.</p><p>For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), the graded-piece and relative vanishing theorems for the ordinary Rees algebra give\n\\[\np_*\\mathcal O_{X'}(-nE)=I^n,\n\\qquad\nR^q p_*\\mathcal O_{X'}(-nE)=0\\quad(q&gt;0).\n\\tag{2.21}\n\\]\nOn any affine open of the Noetherian base, the Rees algebra is a Noetherian graded algebra generated in degree one. Its sufficiently high graded pieces recover the sections of its associated sheaf on Proj, and the higher cohomology of those twists vanishes. A finite affine cover gives one common threshold; see [22, Tags 0AG6 and 0AG7]. In particular, (2.21) uses ordinary powers \\(I^n\\), not their integral closures, and makes no assertion about small \\(n\\).</p><p>By the projection formula, Leray, and Serre vanishing for the ample line bundle \\(A-E\\),\n\\[\nH^1(X,I^n\\otimes L^n)\n=\nH^1\\!\\left(X',\\mathcal O_{X'}(n(A-E))\\right)\n=\n0\n\\tag{2.22}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(n\\).</p><p>The exact sequence for \\(I^n\\) consequently gives a surjection from \\(H^0(X,L^n)\\) onto all local coefficient data modulo \\(I^n\\). The constant projective coordinate trivializes \\(L\\) on the affine chart. Finite-colength local quotients are unchanged by completion, so these algebraic classes are also the formal coefficient data used in the statement.</p><p>Every displayed generator of \\(I\\) has weight \\(R\\). Hence, in these trivializations,\n\\[\nI^n\\subseteq\n\\{\\text{local series of weight at least }nR\\}.\n\\tag{2.23}\n\\]\nSurjectivity modulo \\(I^n\\) therefore implies surjectivity onto the smaller quotient consisting of the desired packets of weight less than \\(nR\\). Equality in (2.23) is not needed.</p><p>Finally, every section of \\(L^n\\), for sufficiently large \\(n\\), is supplied by a homogeneous degree-\\(n\\) polynomial in the projective embedding coordinates. This follows from Serre vanishing applied to the homogeneous ideal sheaf of \\(X\\) in its projective space. Such a polynomial restricts on the affine chart to one of \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(nR\\). Taking \\(H=nR\\) and undoing the filtered Taylor-polynomial coordinate change proves (2.2).</p><p>The divisibility requirement depends only on the weights. The remaining threshold for \\(H\\) is allowed to depend on the fixed points.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “Weighted transverse multiplicity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any coordinate normal basis \\(A\\) and any \\(D\\)-field normal basis \\(B\\), each of cardinality \\(k\\), \\( \\prod_{a\\in A}\\rho_a \\le 2k!\\,\\varepsilon^{-k}\\prod_{b\\in B}\\kappa_b. \\) The bound is uniform in the equations …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This lemma uses an independent local setting.</p><p class=\"li\">Give the coordinates \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_d\\) positive rational degree weights \\(\\rho_1,\\ldots,\\rho_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On an open subset of \\(\\mathbb C^d\\), let \\(D_1,\\ldots,D_d\\) be commuting analytic vector fields forming a frame, and assign them positive rational costs \\(\\kappa_1,\\ldots,\\kappa_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and \\(N&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that polynomials \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_r\\) have weighted degree at most \\(N\\), and that \\(Z\\) is an irreducible component of their common zero set of codimension \\(k\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  D^\\gamma f_\\ell|_Z=0\n  \\qquad\\text{whenever}\\qquad\n  \\sum_{b=1}^d\\kappa_b\\gamma_b&lt;\\varepsilon N.\n  \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A coordinate normal basis \\(A\\) is an index set for coordinate vector fields whose images form a basis of the ambient tangent space modulo \\(T_Z\\) at a general smooth point. A \\(D\\)-field normal basis \\(B\\) is defined in the same way using the \\(D_b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For any coordinate normal basis \\(A\\) and any \\(D\\)-field normal basis \\(B\\), each of cardinality \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\prod_{a\\in A}\\rho_a\n\\le\n2k!\\,\\varepsilon^{-k}\\prod_{b\\in B}\\kappa_b.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nThe bound is uniform in the equations, the component \\(Z\\), and the analytic frame; no lower threshold for \\(N\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a smooth point \\(x\\) of \\(Z\\) where both normal-basis conditions hold and which lies on no other irreducible component of the common zero set. Flowing from \\(Z\\) along the fields indexed by \\(B\\) gives local analytic coordinates \\((z,b)\\), where \\(z\\) parametrizes \\(Z\\) and \\(b=(b_1,\\ldots,b_k)\\) consists of transverse flow parameters. Commutativity implies that the chosen fields are the derivatives in the \\(b\\)-directions.</p><p>Thus (2.3) says that in\n\\[\nf_\\ell(z,b)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^k}f_{\\ell,\\alpha}(z)b^\\alpha,\n\\]\nevery coefficient function of \\(B\\)-weight less than \\(\\varepsilon N\\) vanishes identically on the chosen neighborhood in \\(Z\\).</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be the affine coordinate slice through \\(x\\) obtained by fixing the coordinates outside \\(A\\). It is transverse to \\(Z\\), and the zero set of the restricted equations is isolated at \\(x\\). The flow parameters \\(b\\) give regular analytic coordinates on \\(S\\); the coordinates \\(z\\) become analytic functions \\(z(b)\\). Substitution cannot generate a missing term of smaller \\(B\\)-weight. Indeed, each surviving summand already contains a monomial \\(b^\\alpha\\) of weight at least \\(\\varepsilon N\\), and its analytic coefficient has only nonnegative powers of \\(b\\).</p><p>Consequently the ideal of the restricted equations is contained in the monomial ideal of weight at least \\(\\varepsilon N\\). Its quotient therefore has length at least\n\\[\n\\#\\left\\{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^k:\n\\sum_{b\\in B}\\kappa_b\\alpha_b&lt;\\varepsilon N\n\\right\\}\n\\ge\n\\frac{(\\varepsilon N)^k}{2k!\\prod_{b\\in B}\\kappa_b}.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nIn fact the factor \\(1/2\\) can be omitted: the simplex\n\\[\n\\left\\{x\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^k:\n\\sum_{b\\in B}\\kappa_bx_b&lt;\\varepsilon N\n\\right\\}\n\\]\nis covered by the disjoint half-open unit cubes\n\\(\\alpha+[0,1)^k\\) indexed by the admissible multi-indices\n\\(\\alpha=\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\). Its volume is\n\\[\n\\frac{(\\varepsilon N)^k}{k!\\prod_{b\\in B}\\kappa_b}.\n\\]\nThus (2.5) holds for every \\(N&gt;0\\). Analytic local coordinates preserve finite length, so no radius or coefficient bound for the flow map is needed.</p><p>In the regular local ring of the \\(k\\)-dimensional slice, the ideal generated by the restricted equations is primary to the maximal ideal. Over the infinite field \\(\\mathbb C\\), \\(k\\) sufficiently general constant linear combinations of these equations still have an isolated common zero at \\(x\\): successive generic combinations avoid the finitely many positive-dimensional components that remain. Their ideal is contained in the original ideal. Their quotient length is therefore an upper bound for the length in (2.5).</p><p>Each of these \\(k\\) combinations has weighted degree at most \\(N\\) in the variables indexed by \\(A\\). Their local intersection length is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{N^k}{\\prod_{a\\in A}\\rho_a}.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nTo establish this bound, choose an integer \\(M_0\\) clearing the denominators of the \\(\\rho_a\\), and make the substitutions\n\\[\nx_a=\\lambda_a+z_a^{M_0\\rho_a}\n\\qquad(a\\in A).\n\\]\nChoose the constants \\(\\lambda_a\\) so that no preimage of the given point is ramified. There are\n\\[\n\\prod_{a\\in A}(M_0\\rho_a)\n\\]\nsuch preimages, each with the same local intersection length. The substituted polynomials have ordinary total degree at most \\(M_0N\\). The ordinary Bézout bound for isolated complete-intersection zeros, with their local multiplicities, bounds the sum of these lengths by \\((M_0N)^k\\); see the paper’s reference [17, Corollary VIII.3, p. 144]. Those multiplicities are local quotient lengths, unchanged by completion [17, Section IV.4.2]. Dividing gives (2.6). This application concerns isolated zeros only and permits other components elsewhere.</p><p>Combining (2.5) and (2.6) proves (2.4). Neither the polynomials nor the possibly varying component enters its constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Proposition 2.3, “Curve inequality.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The successive choices in (2.9) can be made with thresholds independent of the interpolation points.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a positive rational number \\(\\sigma\\) sufficiently small that\n  \\[\n  (1+3\\sigma)^{m+1}K(w_0/v_0)\\theta^m&lt;1,\n  \\qquad\n  (1+3\\sigma)^mK\\theta^m&lt;1,\n  \\qquad\n  (1+\\sigma)\\theta&lt;1.\n  \\tag{2.7}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(v_i=w_i/\\theta\\) for \\(i&gt;0\\), \\(L_0=m+2\\), and\n  \\[\n  C_*=\\max_{1\\le k\\le m}2k!(L_0/\\sigma)^k.\n  \\tag{2.8}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the positive rational weights successively so that, for every pair of subsets\n  \\(A,B\\subseteq\\{0,\\ldots,m\\}\\) of the same cardinality between \\(1\\) and \\(m\\),\n  \\[\n  \\prod_{a\\in A}w_a&gt;C_*\\prod_{b\\in B}v_b\n  \\tag{2.9}\n  \\]\n  whenever a positive index differs and the largest positive index in \\(A\\mathbin{\\triangle}B\\) belongs to \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a_j=(1,c_{j1},\\ldots,c_{jm})\\), \\(0\\le j&lt;K\\), have pairwise distinct \\(c_{ji}\\) across \\(j\\) for each \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A curve means an integral algebraic curve over \\(\\mathbb C\\). For a curve \\(C\\) whose generic point lies in the affine coordinate chart, use its smooth projective model to define\n  \\[\n  \\deg_W C\n  =\n  \\sum_P\n  \\max\\left\\{\n  0,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P Y}{w_0},\n  -\\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P X_1}{w_1},\n  \\ldots,\n  -\\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P X_m}{w_m}\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\tag{2.10}\n  \\]\n  The order of an identically zero coordinate is \\(+\\infty\\), so it contributes no pole.</p><p class=\"li\">At a branch \\(P\\) through \\(a_j\\), put\n  \\[\n  h_P=\n  \\min\\left\\{\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P(Y-1)}{v_0},\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P(X_1-c_{j1}-\\log Y)}{v_1},\n  \\ldots,\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P(X_m-c_{jm}-\\log Y)}{v_m}\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\tag{2.11}\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\log Y\\) is its formal expansion at \\(Y=1\\). All entries have positive order, possibly infinite, and their minimum is finite on a nonconstant curve.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The successive choices in (2.9) can be made with thresholds independent of the interpolation points. With these weights, every such curve satisfies\n\\[\n\\deg_W C\\ge(1+\\sigma)\\sum_{P\\mapsto a_j}h_P,\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nwhere the sum includes all branches through the prescribed points.</p>", "proof": "<p>The choice of \\(\\sigma\\) is possible by (2.1). The successive choice in (2.9) is also possible. If the largest differing positive index is \\(i\\), the ratio in (2.9) is \\(w_i\\) times a positive expression in the already chosen lower weights and the fixed zeroth weights. Every higher index occurs in both sets or in neither; in the former case its ratio is \\(w_j/v_j=\\theta\\). Hence the expression is independent of all higher weights. There are finitely many pairs of sets. A sufficiently large choice of \\(w_i\\) enforces all conditions whose largest differing index is \\(i\\), and subsequent choices do not disturb them. This construction involves no interpolation point.</p><p>There is nothing to prove if \\(C\\) misses all the prescribed points. Suppose instead that (2.12) fails.</p><p>For every sufficiently large auxiliary integer \\(N\\), linear algebra gives a nonzero polynomial \\(F_N\\) of \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(N\\) whose expansions at all the points have \\(V\\)-order at least \\((1+3\\sigma)N\\). Indeed, the number of its coefficients has leading term\n\\[\n\\frac{N^{m+1}}{(m+1)!\\prod_{a=0}^m w_a},\n\\]\nwhereas the number of conditions has leading term\n\\[\n\\frac{K(1+3\\sigma)^{m+1}N^{m+1}}\n{(m+1)!\\prod_{a=0}^m v_a}.\n\\]\nTheir comparison is the first inequality in (2.7). This requires no independence among the conditions.</p><p>On \\(Y\\ne0\\), consider the commuting frame\n\\[\nD_0=Y\\partial_Y+\\sum_{i=1}^m\\partial_{X_i},\n\\qquad\nD_i=\\partial_{X_i}\\quad(1\\le i\\le m).\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\nIt preserves polynomial \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(N\\). In the formal coordinates of (2.2),\n\\[\nD_0=(1+t)\\partial_t,\\qquad D_i=\\partial_{u_i}.\n\\]\nThus differentiation by \\(D_a\\) decreases weighted order by at most \\(v_a\\). Every \\(D^\\gamma F_N\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_a v_a\\gamma_a\\le\\sigma N\n\\]\nretains order at least \\((1+2\\sigma)N\\) at each point. On a branch \\(P\\), its order is consequently at least \\((1+2\\sigma)Nh_P\\).</p><p>A polynomial of \\(W\\)-degree at most \\(N\\) restricts to a rational function on the complete model of \\(C\\) with total pole degree at most \\(N\\deg_W C\\). Since (2.12) is assumed to fail, the total zero order just obtained exceeds this pole bound. The divisor equality for a nonzero rational function therefore forces\n\\[\nD^\\gamma F_N|_C=0\n\\qquad\n\\left(\\sum_a v_a\\gamma_a\\le\\sigma N\\right).\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\nThe branch-order assertions hold for formal power series: each monomial has order at least its weight times \\(h_P\\), and cancellation can only increase the order.</p><p>Put \\(\\delta=\\sigma N/L_0\\). For \\(0\\le r\\le L_0\\), let \\(V_r\\) be the common zero set in \\(Y\\ne0\\) of the derivatives of cost at most \\(r\\delta\\). Let \\(C^\\circ\\) be the part of \\(C\\) in this affine open set. The \\(V_r\\) form a decreasing chain, and (2.14) puts \\(C^\\circ\\) in \\(V_{L_0}\\).</p><p>Choose an irreducible component of \\(V_{L_0}\\) containing \\(C^\\circ\\), and extend it backwards to nested irreducible components of the earlier \\(V_r\\). Every selected component has dimension between \\(1\\) and \\(m\\): it contains a curve and lies in the proper zero set of \\(F_N\\). Since there are \\(L_0+1=m+3\\) levels, two adjacent selected components coincide. Denote this persistent component by \\(Z\\), and write \\(k=\\operatorname{codim}Z\\).</p><p>Take the derivatives of cost at most \\(r\\delta\\) as the equations of the earlier of these two levels. Any further derivative of cost less than \\(\\delta\\) vanishes along \\(Z\\), because the fields commute and \\(Z\\) is also a component at the next level. Lemma 1.1, with \\(\\varepsilon=\\sigma/L_0\\), gives\n\\[\n\\prod_{a\\in A}w_a\\le C_*\\prod_{b\\in B}v_b\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\nfor every coordinate normal basis \\(A\\) and every frame normal basis \\(B\\). The equations and \\(Z\\) may vary with \\(N\\); the uniformity in Lemma 1.1 permits this use.</p><p>We show that \\(Y\\) is constant on \\(Z\\), working on a common smooth open subset where all the normal-basis conditions hold. Suppose first that some positive coordinate direction is nontangent to \\(Z\\), and choose the largest such index \\(i\\). Every larger positive index occurs in neither kind of normal basis, since \\(D_j=\\partial_{X_j}\\) for \\(j&gt;0\\). Some coordinate normal basis \\(A\\) contains \\(i\\). If a frame normal basis \\(B\\) omitted \\(i\\), the largest positive index where the sets differ would be \\(i\\), so (2.9) would contradict (2.15). Thus every frame normal basis contains \\(D_i\\).</p><p>The other frame vectors consequently fail to span the normal quotient. Their ambient span is\n\\[\n\\ker(dX_i-dY/Y).\n\\]\nFor an ambient hyperplane \\(Q\\) and a tangent space \\(T\\), the image of \\(Q\\) in the quotient by \\(T\\) fails to be surjective precisely when \\(T\\subseteq Q\\). Hence\n\\[\ndX_i=dY/Y\\qquad\\text{on }Z.\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]</p><p>This identity forces \\(Y\\) to be constant on the algebraic variety \\(Z\\). If \\(Y\\) were nonconstant, choose a smooth point where \\(dY\\ne0\\) and intersect with general algebraic hyperplanes through that point until a curve remains on which \\(Y\\) is nonconstant. On its smooth complete model, (2.16) is an identity of meromorphic differentials. A nonconstant meromorphic function \\(Y\\) has a zero or pole, where \\(dY/Y\\) has nonzero residue \\(\\operatorname{ord}_P Y\\). An exact differential \\(dX_i\\) has zero residue at every point, a contradiction. It follows also that \\(X_i\\) is constant, since \\(dX_i=0\\) in characteristic zero.</p><p>If instead every positive coordinate direction is tangent, the \\(m\\) independent vectors \\(\\partial_{X_i}\\) all lie in the tangent space of \\(Z\\). Properness forces that tangent space to be their span, and \\(dY=0\\) on \\(Z\\) again.</p><p>Thus \\(Y\\) is constant on \\(Z\\). Since \\(C^\\circ\\subseteq Z\\) meets a prescribed point with \\(Y=1\\), one has \\(Y=1\\) on \\(C^\\circ\\), and hence on \\(C\\) by density.</p><p>It remains to rule out a violating curve in that fiber. Work with \\(C^\\circ\\) in the affine fiber \\(Y=1\\), while computing orders and degrees on the complete model of \\(C\\). The degree weights are \\(w_i\\), the order weights are \\(v_i=w_i/\\theta\\), and\n\\[\nh_P=\\min_i\\frac{\\operatorname{ord}_P(X_i-c_{ji})}{v_i}.\n\\]\nChoose a new auxiliary polynomial in these \\(m\\) variables. The ratio of the number of vanishing conditions to the number of coefficients tends to\n\\[\nK(1+3\\sigma)^m\\theta^m&lt;1\n\\]\nby the second inequality in (2.7). Thus, for sufficiently large \\(N\\), there is a nonzero polynomial\n\\(G_N(X_1,\\ldots,X_m)\\) of weighted degree at most \\(N\\) and order at least \\((1+3\\sigma)N\\) at every center.</p><p>An ordinary partial derivative of cost at most \\(\\sigma N\\) still has order at least \\((1+2\\sigma)N\\) at every center and degree at most \\(N\\). The same zero–pole comparison gives\n\\[\n\\partial_X^\\gamma G_N|_C=0\n\\qquad\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^m v_i\\gamma_i\\le\\sigma N\\right).\n\\]\nFor \\(m=1\\), this is impossible: a nonzero polynomial on the affine line cannot vanish on a curve.</p><p>Suppose \\(m\\ge2\\). For \\(0\\le r\\le L_0\\), let \\(U_r\\) be the common zero set in this fiber of the partial derivatives of \\(G_N\\) of cost at most \\(r\\delta\\), with \\(\\delta=\\sigma N/L_0\\). The curve \\(C^\\circ\\) lies in \\(U_{L_0}\\). Choose nested irreducible components containing \\(C^\\circ\\) backwards through this chain. Their dimensions lie between \\(1\\) and \\(m-1\\), since \\(G_N\\ne0\\), so two adjacent components coincide in a component \\(Z'\\). Its codimension in the fiber satisfies \\(1\\le k\\le m-1\\).</p><p>For the equations at the earlier of these two levels, every further partial derivative of cost less than \\(\\delta\\) vanishes on \\(Z'\\). Lemma 1.1 again gives (2.15), with the same \\(C_*\\) and normal-basis index sets contained in \\(\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\).</p><p>Here the coordinate and frame bases are the same. They must have a unique index set: two different basis sets could be ordered so that the largest differing index belongs to \\(A\\), contradicting (2.9). The codimension is positive, so this unique normal basis has some index \\(i\\). The other coordinate directions fail to span the normal quotient; hence the tangent space is contained in \\(\\ker dX_i\\), and \\(X_i\\) is constant on \\(Z'\\).</p><p>Coordinatewise distinctness shows that \\(Z'\\) meets at most one prescribed point. Every prescribed point of \\(C\\) belongs to \\(C^\\circ\\subseteq Z'\\), so the same holds for \\(C\\). Let that point be \\(a_j\\).</p><p>Choose a coordinate \\(X_l\\) nonconstant on \\(C\\). The nonzero rational function \\(X_l-c_{jl}\\) has total pole degree at most \\(w_l\\deg_W C\\), and at each branch through \\(a_j\\) it has order at least \\((w_l/\\theta)h_P\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\deg_W C\n\\ge\\theta^{-1}\\sum_{P\\mapsto a_j}h_P\n&gt;\n(1+\\sigma)\\sum_{P\\mapsto a_j}h_P,\n\\]\nby the last inequality in (2.7). This contradiction proves (2.12).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.4, “Determinant comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The approximation and interpolation assumptions cannot hold simultaneously if \\( g>0,\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}<g,\\qquad cL>1+E_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a real \\(\\nu&gt;2\\), integers \\(m,K\\ge1\\), positive rational numbers \\(w_0,v_0,\\theta\\), and integers \\(p_i,q_i\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), with\n  \\[\n  q_i\\ge2,\\qquad p_i\\ne0,\\qquad\n  \\left|\\pi-\\frac{p_i}{q_i}\\right|\\le q_i^{-\\nu}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  w_i=\\lceil\\log q_i\\rceil,\\qquad\n  r_i=\\frac{2\\mathrm i p_i}{q_i},\\qquad\n  \\omega=2\\pi\\mathrm i,\\qquad \\mathrm i^2=-1.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  Assume \\(m,K,w_0,v_0,\\theta,w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\) satisfy the hypotheses and successive weight thresholds of Proposition 1, with centers \\(c_{ji}=jr_i\\), \\(0\\le j&lt;K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  w\\alpha=\\sum_{i=1}^m w_i\\alpha_i,\n  \\qquad\n  w_*=\\min_{1\\le i\\le m}w_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(F_0&gt;2/\\theta\\), and set\n  \\[\n  T_i=\\left\\lceil\\frac{F_0w_i}{v_0}\\right\\rceil,\n  \\qquad\n  G_i(t)=\\sum_{1\\le k&lt;T_i}\\frac{(-1)^{k+1}t^k}{k}.\n  \\tag{3.2}\n  \\]\n  Empty sums are zero.</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive integer \\(H\\), take columns indexed by monomials\n  \\[\n  P(Y,X)=Y^hX^\\alpha,\\qquad\n  h\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\quad\n  \\alpha\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^m,\\quad\n  w_0h+w\\alpha\\le H,\n  \\]\n  and rows indexed by\n  \\[\n  \\rho=(j,s,\\beta),\\qquad\n  0\\le j&lt;K,\\quad\n  s\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\quad\n  \\beta\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^m,\\quad\n  v_0s+\\frac{w\\beta}{\\theta}&lt;H.\n  \\]\n  The matrix entry is the coefficient of \\(t^su^\\beta\\) in\n  \\[\n  P\\bigl(1+t,jr_1+G_1(t)+u_1,\\ldots,jr_m+G_m(t)+u_m\\bigr).\n  \\tag{3.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M=M_H\\) be the number of rows and define\n  \\[\n  \\bar b=\\bar b_H=\\frac{1}{MH}\\sum_{\\rho=(j,s,\\beta)}w\\beta.\n  \\tag{3.4}\n  \\]\n  A nonzero square minor using every row, when chosen, is denoted \\(\\Delta_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\Lambda=4\\log2,\\qquad c=\\frac{\\log2}{4},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E_{\\mathrm{ar}}\n  =\n  \\frac{\\Lambda F_0m}{v_0}\n  +\\Lambda\\sum_{i=1}^m\\frac1{w_i}\n  +\\frac{\\theta}{w_*},\n  \\tag{3.6}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E_{\\mathrm{tr}}\n  =\n  \\frac{\\nu}{F_0}\n  +\\frac{\\log2}{v_0}\n  +\\frac{\\log4+\\log(2K)+\\nu}{w_*},\n  \\tag{3.12}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E_{\\mathrm{hol}}\n  =\n  \\frac{100K}{w_0}\n  +\\frac{\\log2}{v_0}\n  +\\frac{\\log(200K)}{w_*},\n  \\qquad\n  E_{\\mathrm{an}}=E_{\\mathrm{tr}}+E_{\\mathrm{hol}}.\n  \\tag{3.15, 3.17}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;A&lt;1\\) and \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\), put\n  \\[\n  g=\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\theta\\bigr)-(1-\\theta),\n  \\qquad\n  L=\\frac{\\eta^2K\\theta^m}{(m+1)v_0A^m}.\n  \\tag{3.18}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All parameters except \\(H\\) remain fixed as \\(H\\to\\infty\\). All logarithms are natural.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The approximation and interpolation assumptions cannot hold simultaneously if\n\\[\ng&gt;0,\\qquad\nE_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}&lt;g,\\qquad\ncL&gt;1+E_{\\mathrm{ar}}+E_{\\mathrm{an}}.\n\\tag{3.19}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For sufficiently large, sufficiently divisible \\(H\\), choose \\(\\Delta_H\\ne0\\) as in Lemma 2.1. Put\n\\[\nD_A(H)=\\#\\{a\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^m:wa\\le AH\\},\n\\qquad\nL_H=\\frac{\\eta^2M}{HD_A(H)}.\n\\]\nThe simplex count in dimension \\(m\\), together with (3.5), gives\n\\[\nD_A(H)\\sim\\frac{(AH)^m}{m!\\prod_iw_i},\n\\qquad\nL_H\\longrightarrow\n\\frac{\\eta^2K\\theta^m}{(m+1)v_0A^m}=L.\n\\]</p><p>Expand \\(\\Delta_H\\) by the row identities in Lemma 2.2. For a choice of terms with indices \\(a_\\rho\\), the product of their scalar factors has modulus at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\left\\{\n-\\nu\\sum_\\rho wa_\\rho+\\nu MH\\bar b+MHE_{\\mathrm{tr}}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(Q_H^M\\) choices. Apply Lemma 2.3 to each.</p><p>Suppose first that at least \\(\\eta M\\) of the indices obey \\(wa\\le AH\\). There are at most \\(D_A(H)\\) such indices, so Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_a n_a^2\n\\ge\n\\frac{(\\eta M)^2}{D_A(H)}\n=\nMHL_H.\n\\]\nThe scalar main exponent\n\\[\n-\\nu\\sum_\\rho w(a_\\rho-\\beta_\\rho)\n\\]\nis nonpositive because \\(a_\\rho\\ge\\beta_\\rho\\). Discarding it leaves the upper bound\n\\[\n-cL_H+E_{\\mathrm{an}}+\\rho_H\n\\]\nafter division by \\(MH\\).</p><p>Otherwise, more than \\((1-\\eta)M\\) indices have \\(wa&gt;AH\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_\\rho wa_\\rho\\ge MHA(1-\\eta).\n\\]\nDiscarding the nonpositive collision term leaves\n\\[\n-\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\bar b\\bigr)\n+E_{\\mathrm{an}}+\\rho_H.\n\\]</p><p>Taking absolute values before summing the determinant expansion yields\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_H|}{MH}\n\\le\nE_{\\mathrm{an}}+\\varepsilon_H\n+\n\\max\\left\\{\n-cL_H,\\,\n-\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\bar b\\bigr)\n\\right\\},\n\\tag{3.20}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_H=\\rho_H+\\frac{\\log Q_H}{H}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nAll bounds are uniform over the row-term choices.</p><p>For the second alternative, \\(\\bar b\\le\\theta\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\bar b\\bigr)-(1-\\bar b)\n&amp;=\n\\nu A(1-\\eta)-1-(\\nu-1)\\bar b\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\n\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\theta\\bigr)-(1-\\theta)\n=g.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nUnder (3.19), both quantities inside the maximum in (3.20), after adding\n\\(E_{\\mathrm{an}}+\\varepsilon_H\\), are strictly less than\n\\[\n-(1-\\bar b)-E_{\\mathrm{ar}}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(H\\). For the first quantity, use \\(L_H\\to L\\) and \\(1-\\bar b\\le1\\); for the second, use the uniform gap \\(g\\).</p><p>This contradicts the arithmetic lower bound of Lemma 2.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Arithmetic lower bound,” together with the full-row-rank construction and row count of Section 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large, sufficiently divisible integers \\(H\\), the matrix (3.3) has full row rank, so a nonzero square minor \\(\\Delta_H\\) using every row can be chosen.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the determinant setting of Proposition 2, without imposing the three final inequalities (3.19).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large, sufficiently divisible integers \\(H\\), the matrix (3.3) has full row rank, so a nonzero square minor \\(\\Delta_H\\) using every row can be chosen. Its size and the row average satisfy\n\\[\nM\\sim\\frac{K\\theta^mH^{m+1}}{(m+1)!\\,v_0\\prod_{i=1}^m w_i},\n\\qquad\n0\\le\\bar b\\le\\theta.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nEvery such minor satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_H|}{MH}\n\\ge\n-(1-\\bar b)-E_{\\mathrm{ar}}.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first omitted term of \\(G_i(t)\\) has weight\n\\[\nv_0T_i\\ge F_0w_i&gt;w_i/\\theta.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nu_i\\longmapsto u_i+G_i(t)-\\log(1+t)\n\\]\nis an invertible substitution preserving the weighted filtration. It induces an invertible map on every finite space of coefficients of weight less than \\(H\\). Thus replacing the logarithms in Proposition 1 by the \\(G_i\\) preserves surjectivity.</p><p>It follows that, for sufficiently large, sufficiently divisible \\(H\\), the matrix (3.3) has full row rank. Choose a square minor \\(\\Delta_H\\ne0\\) using every row.</p><p>The elementary lattice count for a simplex gives (3.5). After scaling by \\(H\\), the lattice boxes of side lengths \\(H^{-1}\\) are Riemann sums for the fixed simplex of volume\n\\[\n\\frac{\\theta^m}{(m+1)!\\,v_0\\prod_iw_i}.\n\\]\nIts boundary has volume zero, so the strict inequality in the row definition does not affect the leading term. Also each row satisfies \\(0\\le w\\beta&lt;\\theta H\\), giving \\(0\\le\\bar b\\le\\theta\\). The limit is taken only after all weights and centers have been fixed.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nL_i=\\operatorname{lcm}(1,\\ldots,T_i-1),\n\\]\ninterpreted as \\(1\\) if \\(T_i=1\\), and put\n\\[\nD_H=\\prod_{i=1}^mL_i^{\\lfloor H/w_i\\rfloor}.\n\\]\nScale a column \\(Y^hX^\\alpha\\) by \\(\\prod_iq_i^{\\alpha_i}\\), and a row \\((j,s,\\beta)\\) by \\(\\prod_iq_i^{-\\beta_i}\\). The scaled entry is zero unless \\(\\alpha\\ge\\beta\\) componentwise; otherwise it is\n\\[\n\\prod_i\\binom{\\alpha_i}{\\beta_i}\n[t^s](1+t)^h\n\\prod_i\\bigl(q_i(jr_i+G_i(t))\\bigr)^{\\alpha_i-\\beta_i}.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\nq_ijr_i=2\\mathrm i jp_i\\in\\mathbb Z[\\mathrm i].\n\\]\nSince \\(G_i\\) has coefficients in \\(L_i^{-1}\\mathbb Z\\), the denominator of this entry divides\n\\(\\prod_iL_i^{\\alpha_i-\\beta_i}\\), which divides \\(D_H\\).</p><p>Multiplying every entry of the scaled matrix by \\(D_H\\) gives a matrix over \\(\\mathbb Z[\\mathrm i]\\) with nonzero determinant. A nonzero Gaussian integer has modulus at least one, so\n\\[\n\\log|\\Delta_H|\n\\ge\n-M\\log D_H\n-\\sum_{\\text{columns }\\alpha}\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\log q_i\n+\\sum_{\\text{rows }\\beta}\\sum_i\\beta_i\\log q_i.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p><p>For each selected column,\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\log q_i\\le w\\alpha\\le H.\n\\]\nMoreover \\(0\\le w_i-\\log q_i&lt;1\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{rows }\\beta}\\sum_i\\beta_i\\log q_i\n\\ge\nMH\\bar b-\\sum_{\\text{rows }\\beta}\\sum_i\\beta_i\n\\ge\nMH\\bar b-\\frac{MH\\theta}{w_*}.\n\\]</p><p>For completeness,\n\\[\n\\log\\operatorname{lcm}(1,\\ldots,n)\\le\\Lambda n,\n\\qquad \\Lambda=4\\log2.\n\\]\nThe factorial valuation formula shows that each prime power in \\((\\ell,2\\ell]\\) contributes its logarithmic prime weight to\n\\(\\log\\binom{2\\ell}{\\ell}\\); all other contributions to the latter are nonnegative. Thus the increase of the log least common multiple across this interval is at most \\(2\\ell\\log2\\). Summing over dyadic intervals up to the smallest power of two above \\(n\\) gives at most \\(4n\\log2\\).</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log D_H}{H}\n\\le\n\\Lambda\\sum_i\\frac{T_i}{w_i}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\Lambda F_0m}{v_0}\n+\\Lambda\\sum_i\\frac1{w_i}.\n\\]\nSubstitution in (3.8), followed by division by \\(MH\\), proves (3.7).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Exact row translation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every row \\(\\rho=(j,s,\\beta)\\) of (3.3) is a sum of at most \\( Q_H=(\\lfloor H\\rfloor+1)^{2m} (\\lfloor H/v_0\\rfloor+1) \\) scalar multiples of row vectors …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the determinant setting of Proposition 2, without imposing (3.19), and choose a minor as in Lemma 2.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(a\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^m\\) and every selected column \\(P\\), define\n  \\[\n  f_{a,P}(z)\n  =\n  [u^a]P(e^z,z+u_1,\\ldots,z+u_m).\n  \\tag{3.9}\n  \\]\n  Regard \\((f_{a,P})_P\\) as a row vector indexed by the selected columns.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(P=Y^hX^\\alpha\\),\n  \\[\n  f_{a,P}(z)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\displaystyle\\binom{\\alpha}{a}e^{hz}z^{|\\alpha|-|a|},\n  &amp;a\\le\\alpha,\\\\\n  0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\tag{3.10}\n  \\]\n  In particular, only \\(a\\) with \\(wa\\le H\\) can contribute. Multi-index binomial coefficients are coordinatewise products.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every row \\(\\rho=(j,s,\\beta)\\) of (3.3) is a sum of at most\n\\[\nQ_H=(\\lfloor H\\rfloor+1)^{2m}\n(\\lfloor H/v_0\\rfloor+1)\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nscalar multiples of row vectors\n\\[\n\\left([t^\\ell]f_{a,P}(j\\omega+\\log(1+t))\\right)_P,\n\\qquad\na\\ge\\beta,\\quad wa\\le H,\\quad0\\le\\ell\\le s.\n\\]\nEach scalar \\(\\xi\\) occurring with index \\(a\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|\\xi|\\le\n\\exp\\{-\\nu w(a-\\beta)+HE_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\}.\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i=j(r_i-\\omega),\n\\qquad\n\\tau_i(t)=G_i(t)-\\log(1+t).\n\\]\nSince \\(e^{j\\omega}=1\\), the substitutions in (3.3) can be written as\n\\[\nY=e^z,\\qquad\nX_i=z+u_i+\\epsilon_i+\\tau_i(t),\n\\qquad\nz=j\\omega+\\log(1+t).\n\\]\nExpanding first in the \\(u_i\\), then in the constants \\(\\epsilon_i\\) and tails \\(\\tau_i\\), gives the exact row vector identity\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{row}_{j,s,\\beta}\n={}&amp;\n\\sum_{\\substack{a\\ge\\beta\\\\wa\\le H}}\n\\binom a\\beta\n\\sum_{0\\le d\\le a-\\beta}\n\\binom{a-\\beta}{d}\\epsilon^{a-\\beta-d}\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\cdot\n\\sum_{k=0}^s\n\\left([t^k]\\prod_i\\tau_i(t)^{d_i}\\right)\n\\left(\n[t^{s-k}]f_{a,P}(j\\omega+\\log(1+t))\n\\right)_P.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(3.13)\n\\]\nAll scalar factors are independent of the column \\(P\\). Thus determinant multilinearity applies directly.</p><p>The product of tails vanishes to order at least \\(\\sum_i d_iT_i\\). Consequently a nonzero coefficient in (3.13) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_i d_iw_i\n\\le\n\\frac{v_0}{F_0}\\sum_i d_iT_i\n\\le\n\\frac{v_0s}{F_0}\n&lt;\n\\frac H{F_0}.\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nOn \\(|t|=1/2\\),\n\\[\n|\\tau_i(t)|\n\\le\n\\sum_{k\\ge T_i}\\frac{2^{-k}}k\n\\le1.\n\\]\nCauchy’s coefficient estimate therefore bounds the tail coefficient in (3.13) by \\(2^k\\le2^s\\).</p><p>The approximation assumptions give\n\\[\n|\\epsilon_i|\n\\le2Kq_i^{-\\nu}\n\\le2Ke^\\nu e^{-\\nu w_i}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\binom a\\beta\\binom{a-\\beta}{d}\\le4^{|a|},\n\\qquad\n|a|\\le H/w_*.\n\\]\nCombining these inequalities and (3.14), each nonzero scalar satisfies\n\\[\n\\log|\\xi|\n\\le\n-\\nu w(a-\\beta)\n+\\frac{\\nu H}{F_0}\n+\\frac{H\\log2}{v_0}\n+\\frac{H(\\log4+\\log(2K)+\\nu)}{w_*}.\n\\]\nA zero scalar obeys the same required bound.</p><p>Finally, each coordinate of \\(a\\) and \\(d\\) is at most \\(H\\), because \\(w_i\\ge1\\), and \\(0\\le k\\le H/v_0\\). This gives the term count (3.11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Collision estimate.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a quantity \\(\\rho_H\\to0\\), independent of all the row choices and of the selected columns, such that \\( |\\det T| \\le \\exp\\left\\{ -c\\sum_a n_a^2+MH(E_{\\mathrm{hol}}+\\rho_H) \\right\\}, \\) where …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the determinant setting of Proposition 2, without imposing (3.19), and the entire functions of Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose one translated test row from each row expansion in Lemma 2.2 and remove its scalar factor.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) be the resulting square matrix, and let \\(n_a\\) be the number of its rows with transverse index \\(a\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a quantity \\(\\rho_H\\to0\\), independent of all the row choices and of the selected columns, such that\n\\[\n|\\det T|\n\\le\n\\exp\\left\\{\n-c\\sum_a n_a^2+MH(E_{\\mathrm{hol}}+\\rho_H)\n\\right\\},\n\\tag{3.16}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nc=\\frac{\\log2}{4},\n\\qquad\nE_{\\mathrm{hol}}\n=\n\\frac{100K}{w_0}\n+\\frac{\\log2}{v_0}\n+\\frac{\\log(200K)}{w_*}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(R=100K\\). Formula (3.10) gives, on \\(|z|\\le R\\),\n\\[\n|f_{a,P}(z)|\n\\le\n\\exp\\left\\{\nH\\left(\\frac R{w_0}+\\frac{\\log(2R)}{w_*}\\right)\n\\right\\}\n=:D_H.\n\\]\nExpand\n\\[\nf_{a,P}(z)=\\sum_{d\\ge0}c_{a,d,P}(z/R)^d.\n\\]\nCauchy’s estimate gives \\(|c_{a,d,P}|\\le D_H\\).</p><p>A row of \\(T\\) is a test of order \\(\\ell\\le s\\) at some center \\(j\\). Since\n\\[\n|j\\omega+\\log(1+t)|\n\\le2\\pi(K-1)+\\log2\n&lt;50K=R/2\n\\qquad(|t|=1/2),\n\\]\nits value on \\((z/R)^d\\) has modulus at most \\(2^\\ell2^{-d}\\).</p><p>Expand the determinant in these power-series rows. The expansion is absolutely convergent: a coefficient determinant has modulus at most \\(M!D_H^M\\), and the sum of the product bounds \\(2^{-d}\\) over all degrees is finite.</p><p>If two rows have the same pair \\((a,d)\\), their coefficient vectors \\((c_{a,d,P})_P\\) agree, so that summand vanishes. Thus every nonzero summand has distinct degrees within each group \\(a\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{rows}}d\\ge\\sum_a\\binom{n_a}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>Use half of the geometric decay for this lower bound and sum the other half freely over all nonnegative degrees. Since\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{rows}}\\ell\\le MH/v_0,\n\\]\nthis gives\n\\[\n|\\det T|\n\\le\nM!D_H^M2^{MH/v_0}\n2^{-\\frac12\\sum_a\\binom{n_a}{2}}\n(1-2^{-1/2})^{-M}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\sum_a n_a=M\\), the asserted bound follows with, for example,\n\\[\n\\rho_H\n=\n\\frac{\n\\log M+\\frac14\\log2-\\log(1-2^{-1/2})\n}{H}.\n\\]\nThe asymptotic formula (3.5) shows \\(\\rho_H\\to0\\) with all other parameters fixed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are positive rational numbers \\(\\theta,A,B,C\\) such that \\( 0<\\theta<A<B<1, \\qquad \\nu(A-\\theta)>1-\\theta, \\qquad C>1, \\qquad B<1/C, \\qquad B<C\\theta<1. \\) One can then choose \\(0<\\eta<1\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a real number \\(\\nu&gt;2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive rational numbers \\(\\theta,A,B,C\\) such that\n\\[\n0&lt;\\theta&lt;A&lt;B&lt;1,\n\\qquad\n\\nu(A-\\theta)&gt;1-\\theta,\n\\qquad\nC&gt;1,\n\\qquad\nB&lt;1/C,\n\\qquad\nB&lt;C\\theta&lt;1.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nOne can then choose \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\) such that\n\\[\ng:=\\nu\\bigl(A(1-\\eta)-\\theta\\bigr)-(1-\\theta)&gt;0.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a rational number \\(b\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac12&lt;b&lt;1-\\frac1\\nu.\n\\]\nFor a sufficiently small positive rational \\(\\delta\\), put\n\\[\n\\theta=1-\\delta,\\qquad A=1-b\\delta.\n\\]\nThen \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;A&lt;1\\), and\n\\[\n\\nu(A-\\theta)-(1-\\theta)\n=\n\\bigl(\\nu(1-b)-1\\bigr)\\delta&gt;0,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n\\theta-A^2=(2b-1)\\delta-b^2\\delta^2&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>It follows that the interval \\((A/\\theta,1/A)\\) is nonempty. Choose a rational \\(C\\) in this interval. Since \\(A&gt;\\theta\\), one has \\(C&gt;1\\); since \\(\\theta&lt;A\\), one also has\n\\[\nC\\theta&lt;\\theta/A&lt;1.\n\\]\nChoose a rational \\(B\\) between \\(A\\) and \\(\\min(1/C,C\\theta)\\). This proves (4.1).</p><p>Finally, the strict inequality at \\(\\eta=0\\) allows a sufficiently small \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) in (4.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Dyadic spacing estimate.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\sum_{q=1}^{\\infty}\\frac1{q^3\\|q\\alpha\\|^2}<\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) be irrational.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\|x\\|=\\min_{p\\in\\mathbb Z}|x-p|\n  \\]\n  for distance to the nearest integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for some \\(c&gt;0\\) and \\(1&lt;\\nu&lt;5/2\\),\n  \\[\n  \\|q\\alpha\\|\\ge cq^{1-\\nu}\n  \\qquad(q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ q\\ge1).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{q=1}^{\\infty}\\frac1{q^3\\|q\\alpha\\|^2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a positive integer \\(K\\), and put\n\\[\nd=c(2K)^{1-\\nu}.\n\\]\nFor \\(K\\le q&lt;2K\\), the points \\(q\\alpha\\) on the circle \\(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) have distance at least \\(d\\) from zero. Two distinct such points also have circle distance at least \\(d\\): apply the hypothesis to their nonzero integer difference, whose absolute value is less than \\(2K\\), and use \\(1-\\nu&lt;0\\).</p><p>On either half-circle starting at zero, arrange the distances in increasing order. Their first distance is at least \\(d\\), and each successive distance increases by at least \\(d\\). Thus these distances are at least\n\\[\nd,2d,3d,\\ldots.\n\\]\nNo positive integer multiple of \\(\\alpha\\) lies at either endpoint of a half-circle. Consequently\n\\[\n\\sum_{K\\le q&lt;2K}\\frac1{q^3\\|q\\alpha\\|^2}\n\\le\n\\frac2{K^3d^2}\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\frac1{j^2}\n=\nO(K^{2\\nu-5}).\n\\]\nThe implied constant depends only on \\(c\\) and \\(\\nu\\). Since \\(2\\nu-5&lt;0\\), summing over\n\\[\nK=1,2,4,\\ldots\n\\]\nproves the assertion.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [11, 1, "cite"], [11, 2, "cite"]]});
